What's New from Python 2.0 to AmigaPython 2.7.18?
=================================================

This document summarises what Amiga users should know when moving from the
classic AmigaPython 2.0 distribution to this 2.7.18 port.  It is not a full
upstream changelog — for that, see:

  https://docs.python.org/2.7/whatsnew/index.html

and the per-release "What's New in Python 2.x" pages (2.1 through 2.7).


Why 2.7.18?
-----------

Python 2.7.18 (April 2020) is the final Python 2 release.  Relative to 2.0
(October 2000) it includes about a decade of language, library, and bugfix
work, plus the last security and build fixes of the 2.x line.


Language highlights since 2.0
-----------------------------

These new featrures were added across 2.1–2.7 (many backported from early Python 3):

* Nested scopes; generators (`yield`); list/dict/set comprehensions
* `True`/`False`/`None` as constants; `bool` type
* New-style classes refined; descriptors, properties, `super`
* Decorators (`@decorator`); class decorators (2.6+)
* `with` statement / context managers (2.5+); multiple managers in one `with` (2.7)
* `try`/`except`/`finally` unified; relative imports; `print` as a function
  via `from __future__ import print_function`
* Set literals `{1, 2}` and dict/set comprehensions (2.7)
* `str.format()` and related formatting improvements
* Integer division future; `long`/`int` unification progress; float `repr`
  improvements
* `memoryview`; better `io` module (C rewrite in 2.7)


Standard library highlights since 2.0
-------------------------------------

* `subprocess`, `sqlite3`, `hashlib`, `hmac`, `json`, `argparse`
* `collections` (`deque`, `defaultdict`, `namedtuple`, `OrderedDict`, `Counter`)
* `itertools`, `functools`, `contextlib`, `abc`, `numbers`
* `email` package overhaul; `urllib2` / `httplib` evolution; `ssl` where built
* `xml.etree.ElementTree`; continued Expat / SAX / DOM support
* Enhanced `unittest`, `logging`, `multiprocessing` (limited usefulness on
  classic Amiga without process fork/threads)
* Many modules renamed or reorganised vs 2.0; old names often remain as
  compatibility shims for a while — prefer current 2.7 names in new code


Amiga-specific changes in this port
-----------------------------------

See also Docs/Amiga/ and the top-level `readme`.

* Builtin `amiga` module: broader POSIX surface (access, isatty, sleep/usleep,
  kill/waitpid/nice, pathconf/fpathconf/sysconf, tempnam, urandom, fsync,
  closerange, gettimeofday/settimeofday, abort, _exit, getppid, …).
* Dos.library helpers formerly in Doslib, plus `_amigados` (ArgParser,
  FIBF_* / SIGBREAKF_* constants, touch, Assign*), are attached to `amiga`.
* Path helpers `amiga.to_unix` / `amiga.from_unix` (in addition to
  `os.path` / amigapath behaviour).
* ASL: `amiga.FileRequest`, `amiga.MessageBox`
* Locale: `amiga.OpenCatalog` → Catalog.`GetString`
* Icons: `amiga.DiskObject` → `tooltypes` / `deftool` / `stacksize` / `PutIcon`
* Still no usable `fork` / `execv*` on classic Amiga; `pipe` and thread-heavy
  APIs remain limited or unavailable depending on build.
* Prefer `os` over importing `amiga` for portable code; use `amiga` only for
  Amiga-only APIs (Dos, ASL, catalogs, icons, crc32, fullpath, path convert).


Compatibility tips when porting scripts from AmigaPython 2.0
------------------------------------------------------------

* Module filenames remain case-sensitive unless `PYTHONCASEOK` is set.
* Prefer `os.path` over hard-coding `amigapath`; path semantics (`:` absolute,
  `//` parent) are unchanged in spirit.
* `string` module functions that were deprecated after 2.0 should use
  str/unicode methods instead.
* Nested scopes and generators mean some old "workarounds" are unnecessary.
* Use `except Exception as e` style carefully: 2.7 still allows
  `except Exception, e` (classic) and supports `as` with a future/import in
  some contexts — stick to one style per file.
* For new CLI tools, prefer `argparse` over older `getopt`/`optparse` patterns
  when convenient; Amiga `Dos.ArgParser` / `amiga.ArgParser` remains ideal for
  Amiga-style ReadArgs templates.


Where to read more
------------------

* Docs/Amiga/readme — index of Amiga module notes
* https://docs.python.org/2.7/ — official Python 2.7 documentation
* Project README / BUILD notes in the Source tree for how this port is built
